[Music]  oil and gas industry started this  fracking boom  [Music]  and at first they were encroaching into  neighborhoods and into backyards not in  our backyard not in anyone's backyard  get the frack out of our lives  now their emissions their pollution that  they release from these sites is a  problem for everyone around the globe  because methane is a very powerful  greenhouse gas  the permian basin in texas  it's by far the most productive field in  the country i think more than half the  country's drill rigs were operating  there over the past few years  and sharon wilson goes out there every  couple months with this camera that can  see methane this invisible pollutant  if you could see the emissions with your  naked eye there never would have been a  fracking boom you cannot see it without  this camera  [Music]  [Applause]  if you think about the fossil fuels that  are down in the ground  one of the main ones is actually methane  and what we refer to as natural gas  is essentially a combination of gases  that come up from the ground that are  mostly methane with a few other gases  thrown in there  methane is a super powerful greenhouse  gas it has about 80 times the global  warming potential of carbon dioxide over  a 20-year period and scientists think  that about a quarter of all the  human-caused warming that we've  experienced to date is because of  methane emissions by humans  we do a lot of different things to put  methane in the atmosphere  raising livestock is a big contributor  growing rice is another one  landfills sewage there's all different  kinds of things  and then of course oil and gas  there's a new unconventional process for  extracting natural gas from shale  in the early 2000s there were these  combination of innovations in how we  drill for oil and gas we sometimes refer  to it as fracking we drill way way deep  past the water table as far as two miles  and we drill horizontally through that  shale  and one of the things that did was it  caused natural gas to be way more  abundant and cheap than it ever was  before we're pulling almost  three-quarters of a billion cubic feet  of natural gas a day fracking has  revolutionized the u.s energy market  natural gas if you burn it in a power  plant it emits about half the carbon of  coal  so that kind of looks like a real  positive for the environment the risk  with that is that we didn't really know  how much methane might be escaping prior  to being burned in a power plant so now  we know that the permian the basin as a  whole is emitting about 2.9 million  metric tons of methane a year  if you think about how much warming that  gas is causing it's about the same as  the entire state of florida all the  human-caused missions and you know one  of our most populous states  [Music]  i was curious about finding some of the  lowest hanging fruit in terms of climate  action  some of the things that the world is  going to have to do to  slow the pace of global warming are  difficult the methane problem in the oil  and gas industry  is relatively tractable it involves  essentially preserving something that  the industry is wasting that it could be  selling for money  so i went out to the permian basin in  may and i drove around for a few days  with sharon wilson  oh these guys are going to be pissed  because i am going to get out and  take a video of their site  [Music]  i'm a certified optical gas imaging  thermographer and a field advocate for  earthworks  i use an optical gas imaging camera it's  manufactured by flir which stands for  forward-looking infrared  it's 100 000  camera that uses infrared sensors to  produce an image that shows methane  i make visible the invisible emissions  out here to prove that the oil and gas  industry is not containing their methane  gas  i'm seeing a lot of emissions coming  from the tank valves  sometimes the industry will say that  steam  that's just an excuse industry makes  they use the same  camera that we use  they don't use it to go find steam  sharon was um  not really an environmentalist she used  to work for an oil services company back  in the day  and she had this beautiful place out in  the country she was raising her boys  there and  she really blamed fracking for  kind of ruining the air and water in her  area and so she became sort of mobilized  to do something about it  when my water turned black  no one came  because  if it has to do with oil and gas  no one's coming  you're on your own  so i didn't want  other people  to go through that  [Music]  we went out towards pecos texas which is  near the heart of where the oil boom has  been kind of happening in the last few  years  a lot of the permian is just miles and  miles away from a public road  and the roads are just kind of built by  the oil companies and off limits to the  public  and so there's a lot of kind of driving  around figuring out how to get places  oh yeah i don't see a sign that's no  trespassing  i don't know what do y'all think  all they can do is come at us on their  four-wheeler with raffle  i didn't know  whether we would find any methane  i mean  most of these emissions are evidence of  something going wrong  maybe we'd strike  out but you know sharon has done this a  lot she was pretty confident  and we found lots of events every day  methane it's just kind of everywhere  so we're out here in the permian basin  at night  we're not far from pecos texas  and across the street from us is a uh  is a well site  um and to the unaided eye it looks uh  pretty normal give you a look  now we'll show you what sharon wilson  sees through her  optical gas imaging camera  that's what the camera sees  is coming up from the top of the flare  an unlit flare  it's confounding because of course  natural gas is a useful fuel that people  pay money for it's not really in  anyone's interest to  waste it  but gas is very difficult to handle it's  it's hard to store it's hard to  transport it's easy for pressure to  build up somewhere and you have to  release it somehow  so in places like the permian basin you  see a lot of giant flares that are  burning natural gas  it sounds kind of like a jet engine  [Music]  if you burn the gas obviously that's not  great for the environment because you're  putting carbon dioxide into the air  but releasing it without burning it is  something like 16 times as bad because  then you're releasing methane rather  than carbon  when a flare goes out it just becomes a  big vent pipe  and it vents methane gas to the  atmosphere  we found leaks both from places she'd  been to before that were still  problematic and new places we just  stumbled across  there's pretty significant emissions  coming from the top  of this row of tanks that you see behind  me  volatile organic compounds and methane  gas  i wouldn't want to live downwind  uh so we put a lot of miles on today  drove around to a lot of sites  and uh  it turns out it's actually pretty easy  to find these emissions if you just look  around  this part of the country with a camera  that can see them  once we have the footage we  submit complaints to the tceq the texas  regulatory agency  i've made  over 240 complaints  and even if they take action it doesn't  last  but until something changes we'll keep  doing this  sharon has probably done more than  anyone else to kind of publicize the  problem in the permian  but sharon's work is  kind of scatter shot in a way because  her goal is to find problems and  publicize them a lot of other people out  there are focused on  what can we do  to clean it up  so environmental defense fund you could  think about what they're doing in the  permian is sort of like dispatching like  a thousand sharon wilson's hitting  hundreds and hundreds of sites in a  systematic way to try to put some kind  of scientific  credibility behind their findings so in  this case it potentially could be a  safety hazard in addition to so i went  to austin texas to visit david lyon  who's running edf's permian project  what have you found so far  we found that emissions in the permian  are very high  we find that about five percent of these  flares are unlit and venting all the  methane to the atmosphere and about  another five percent have combustion  problems  i i did expect some flares to be unlit  but  was shocked that consistently we're  finding at least five percent our unlit  inventing so showing that this is a  ubiquitous major problem in in the basin  what's the hope that you'll be able to  accomplish  so really we have two goals one is to  have the data inform regulators so they  can design effective policies we also  want to have actions by the operators in  the permian so they would make changes  to their sites which would  would minimize the occurrence of those  kinds of issues in the future  the industry is kind of under attack  from all sides right now  last year the french government  essentially vetoed a seven billion  dollar deal to import liquefied natural  gas from the permian because of concerns  about the greenhouse gas  profile  and that really sent a shockwave through  the energy industry  and at the same time these companies are  catching it from their investors and  lenders so wall street banks and asset  managers are really pushing these  companies to do something about their  emissions profile  if you look at what oil companies are  saying publicly they are  saying almost uniformly that they're  very focused on this problem we're wide  awake about the dangers of climate  change we put very stringent targets on  methane it's absolutely critical that we  as a company get this right and frankly  we as an industry get this right we are  ready to curb our emissions and we will  do it together  for a better and sustainable future  so bp they've been doing a lot to change  up how their whole infrastructure works  there to reduce the amount of methane  that they're leaking  reduce the amount of gas that they're  flaring they say that this changeover is  going to cost upwards of a billion  dollars but that it'll ultimately pay  for itself through additional sales of  gas  for oil and gas the the pollutant is the  product so that's one of the reasons  that edf focuses on oil and gas methane  emissions it's a win-win situation the  planet benefits from less greenhouse gas  emissions but at the same time the  operators benefit because they have more  efficient operations and can often sell  more product  [Music]  the industry is in a period of  retrenchment right now  there was just an absolute boom times a  few years ago when people were just  drilling like crazy  and that kind of rush contributed to the  scale of the waste and emissions that we  saw  the pandemic and lower oil prices have  slowed that down historic drop in crude  oil prices at one point the price fell  to a shocking negative 37 apparel and  even as prices now climb back up the  industry is sort of pledging not to go  crazy again  but in a few years  when the next boom happens either in the  permian or somewhere else and there's  real money to be made  are they going to show the same  restraint that they're pledging right  now  or are they going to focus on the short  term bottom line which is let's just  drill as fast as we can  and who cares if some methane leaks into  the  air people in the industry  often say that they're actually working  on this methane problem  uh does that make you feel better do you  feel like we're on the right track no  i've been hearing that for over a decade  now i've been hearing their promises for  over a decade  it's like  forgiving your cheating lover one more  time  [Music]  you

